From b22d1032b2ac5d6bff58bb0da4f87891a5bb7e77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 10:33:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Finish item 3-8a's consumers, price them at the read, and
 close the item

The dual-representation local built in the previous change was a 2.1%
regression because every read went through the variable's readable
expression, which boxes the raw half back up. This adds the three
consumers that can take a raw double, and then the counter that should
have come first.

The consumers. The guarded arithmetic tree offers a speculative local as
a leaf -- OrderedNode already IS a raw double, a flag and a fallback, so
the shape needed nothing invented -- snapshotted into three CLR locals at
the leaf's own postorder position, because a later leaf can write the
same local. The element read and the element write each emit two arms
over item 3-0's GetElementByNumber / SetElementByNumber and the ordinary
indexer.

Two defects found building them.

A speculative leaf is NOT IsLeaf. That flag means "the saved operand is
the value whichever way the test went", true of an ordinary guarded leaf
which saved the JSValue it was handed, and false of this one, whose slot
is deliberately stale exactly while the flag is up. A tree falling to its
generic arm read a value several increments old -- x++ three times then
x + tail answered "0!" instead of "3!", with no exception and no NaN.

And the leaf was nearly unreachable. Eligibility is
CountOperators - 1 + CountNativeLeaves >= 1, and a speculative local
counts as neither, so `c + p.v` -- the shape the population is made of --
was refused for having no saving to make. Counted as its own term, which
is self-gating: with the switch off no variable carries a flag.

The counter. JSNumber.CreateSpeculativeRead is a fourth factory entry
beside CreateLiteral and CreateConversion, attributing a box at the read.
It settles the item in one line: NavierStokes mints 393 705 boxes reading
a speculative local and the whole item removes about 5 300. The 835 584
steps it takes off Increment mostly save no allocation, because they are
x[++i] and the result is boxed to be an index either way.

Measured, one build, the switch the only difference: 1.021x storage
alone, 1.017x with the tree leaf and the element read, 1.012x with the
element write. The item is closed as a measured regression and stays off
by default; what makes it lose is the read/write ratio of the code it
targets, which is a property of the workload rather than of how many
consumers the compiler grows.

Tests. 20 read-path fixtures, every one on both settings of the switch.
Three of them initially passed against the broken emitter -- two because
the tree was refused before the leaf ran, one because its `i = "2"` was
provably non-numeric and defeated the local's candidacy at compile time
-- so each was then re-checked by breaking the emitter deliberately and
confirming it fails. The ordering fixture could not be repaired and
became a pair asserting why: to disturb the index from a receiver a
getter must close over it, and a captured binding is a JSVariable cell,
which is not a candidate for either numeric tier.

Full engine suite green on both settings: 1 191 compiler, 4 571
integration, 2 103 built-ins, plus runtime, core, parser, modules,
storage and CLR.
